BOX-01 - The Identity and Access Management (IAM) Team implements AC-6 by ensuring users access data necessary to their role. The organization uses Role Based Access (RBAC) with granular permission management. This occurs every 30 to 90 days (Monthly or Quarterly). Results are reviewed by the IAM Team. Evidence includes the Spreadsheet, the IT Ticket and the Resolution Log.
BOX-02 - The Identity and Access Management (IAM) Team implements AC-21 by making certain the organization facilitaes informationa sharing by enabling authorized information sharing from authorized users to determine whether access authorizations assigned to a sharing partner match the information's access and use restrictions. The organization uses (DLP) Microsoft Purview to encrypt and block unathorized senistive data from leaving the network. This occurs Quarterly, every 30 days. Results are reviewed by the IAM Team. Evidence includes the Automated-DeProvisioning, User Access Review (UAR) Signoff Report and the Ticket Match.
BOX-03 - The Identity and Access Management (IAM) Team implements AC-2(11) by enforcing and specifying usage conditions to increase user accountability and enable effective account monitoring. The organization uses Just-In-Time (JIT) Access to lock or entirely disable an accounts until a user requests access for a specific change window. This typically occurs every 1 to 4 hours. Results are reviewed by the Team Leads and System/Data Owners. Evidence includes The Ticket Matching Reconciliation Sheet, PAM Session Review Log and Quarterly User Access Review (UAR) Sign-Off.
BOX-04 - The The Information Security (InfoSec) Team implements MP-3 by marking system media indicating the distribution limitations, handling caveats and applicable security markings (if any) of the information. The organization uses Microsoft Purview Information Protection to enforce that the data is inc compliance with the control. The occurs every 1 to 7 days (Weekly). Results are reviewed by the InfoSec Team. Evidence includes Incremental Check Logic, Community Validation and Defualt Out-Of-The-Box Setting. 
BOX-05 - The The Information Security (InfoSec) Team implements MP-4 by physically control and securely storing media within controlled areas. The organization uses physical safes or lockboxes to securely store physical components inside locked desks, drawers, safes or specialized media cabinents. This occurs at least once every 12 months (annually) or whenever a major system infrastucture change occurs. Results are reviewed by the InfoSec Team. Evidence includes Media Inventory Spreadsheets, Facility Photos and Cryptographic Key Management Logs.
BOX-06 - The Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) Team implements CA-2 by selecting the appropriate assessment team for the type of assessment to be conducted. The organization uses ServiceNOW Integrated Risk Management (IRM) to automate contol system workflows and sends evidence requests to system owners and maps assessment results directly to the NIST 800-53 framework. Periodicly, scheduled by humans can occur Weekly, Quarterly and Monthly. Results are reviewed by the ERM Team. Evidence includes Assesor Independence Attestation, System Security Plan (SSP) Alignment Review and Vulnerability Scan Reports. 
BOX-07 - The Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) Team implements CA-7 by requiring a continuous monitoring program to track the security posture, vulnerabilities, and changes to the system in real-time. The organization uses Splunk Enterprise Security (ES) to correlate data from firewalls endpoints and applications to detect security incidents in real time. This occurs continuously within milliseconds of an event happening with a network. There is no schedule. Evidence includes Log Sample Extraction, Skipped Search Reports and Notable Event Lifecycle History.
BOX-08 - The IT Compliance Team implements AU-2 by identifying the types of events that the system is capable of logging in support of the audit fuction. The organization uses Windows Event Log to generate core system events. The occurs in real time within milliseconds of an activity occuring within the operating system. Results are reviewed by the IT Compliance Team. Evidence includes Log Retention Policy, Network Time Protocol (NTP) Settings and SIEM Ingestion Dashboard Screenshot.
BOX-09 - The IT Compliance Team implements AU-8 by generating time stamps for audit records using internal system clocks. The organization uses Network Time Protocol which internal clocks must synchronize to a trusted, highly accurrate atomic time standard, to ensure compliance. This occurs every 64 seconds regarding Initial Startup and every 17 minutes for Stable Operation. Results are reviewed by the IT Compliance Team. Evidence includes The Enterprise "Network Synchronization Standard," Chrony Configuration File Output and Windows Time Diagnostic Command Output.
BOX-10 - The Information Security (InfoSec) Team implements CM-3 by documenting and determining the types of changes to the system that are configuration-controlled. The organization uses IT Service Management (ITSM) tools such as BMC Helix/Freshservice which is an alternative enterprise platform for tracking logging and approving system changes. This occurs in real time. 24/7/365. Standard/minor changes occur weekly based on developer sprint cycles. Results are reviewed by the InfoSec Team. Evidence includes The Change Request Ticket, Security Impact Analysis (SIA), Electronic CAB Approvals and Digital Signatures. 
BOX-11 - Executive Risk and Governance Team implements CA-6 by assigning a senior official as the authorizing official for the system. The organization uses an enterprise Governance, Risk, and Compliance (eGRC) platform such as Xacta (by Telos) which visualizes control inheritance and auto-generates the exact risk packages needed for federal sign-off. The aforementioned is standard for the U.S. Department of Defense (DoD) and the Intelligence Community. For Full Security Re-Authorization Cycle, this occurs every 3 Years, for Continuous Monitoring & ConMon Reporting Cadence, this occurs Monthly and Real Time and for Core Software Updates & AI Capability Enhancements, this occurs Quarterly and Biennially. Results are reviewed by the Executive Risk and Governance Team. Evidence includes The Signed Authorization to Operate (ATO) Official Letter, The Security Assessment Report (SAR) and Active Plan of Action and Milestones (POA&M) Logs.
BOX-12 - The Governance and Compliance Team implements SR-1 by developing, documenting, and disseminating a Supply Chain Risk Management (SCRM) Policy that addresses purpose, scope, roles, responsibilities, management commitment, coordination among organizational entities, and compliance. This policy must be formally reviewed and updated every 12 months or upon significant changes to the organization’s supply chain infrastructure. This occurs continuously and depending on the event it could happen annually, such as Vendor Re-Assessments. Results are reviewed by thr Governance and Compliance Team. Evidence includes Meeting Minutes, Approved Policy Documents and System Logs from the GRC Tool.
BOX-13 - The Governance and Compliance Team implements SR-2 by developing a formal Supply Chain Risk Management (SCRM) Plan that defines the strategy, funding, resources, risk tolerance, and assessment methodologies for managing supply chain risks. The plan must incorporate a standardized vendor tiering structure and be reviewed, updated, and approved by executive leadership at least annually. Results are reviewed by the Governance and Compliance Team. Evidence includes Executive Sign-off Logs, Completed Vendor Intake Forms and Conditional Approval Tickets.
BOX-14 - The Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) & Corporate Governance Teams implement PM-1 by establisheing, documenting, and maintaining a formal Responsible AI Governance Program. This program requires that prior to the development, training, procurement, or production deployment of any machine learning model or generative AI application, the system owner must submit a standardized AI Algorithmic Impact Assessment (AIA) to the AI Governance Committee. The AIA must explicitly evaluate and document potential risks related to data provenance, algorithmic bias, model explainability, and safety boundaries. System approval and model onboarding are strictly prohibited until the AI Governance Committee formally reviews, signs off, and registers the system in the Central AI Inventory. This occurs when an AI project changes and scheduled governance reviews, which are continuous and on an annual basis. Results are reviewed by the Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) & Corporate Governance Teams. Evidence includes Governance Frameworks, Data Provenance and Adversarial Defense. 
BOX-15 - The Corporate Training GRC Team implements PS-8 by provideing mandatory, role-based Responsible AI and security awareness training to all personnel prior to granting access to corporate AI assets, and at least annually thereafter. The training program must incorporate specific instruction on AI ethics, data provenance, algorithmic bias mitigation, prompt injection prevention, and intellectual property/data leakage risks. For high-risk roles (including machine learning engineers, data scientists, and prompt designers), advanced technical training on model robustness, adversarial attacks, and fairness metrics is strictly required. Training completion metrics must be tracked, and non-compliant personnel must have their AI tool access automatically revoked within 15 days of training expiration. This occurs As-Needed, such as Milestone Driven Triggers and Scheduled Triggers, such as Calendar-Based. Results are reviewed by the Corporate Training GRC Team. Evidence includes LMS Course SCORM Package/Storyboard PDF, New Hire Onboarding Cross-Reference Log and Active Directory / Identity Provider (IdP) Group Roster.
